{"name":"Azores Blue","hex":"#0085a7","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Azores","blocks":[{"text":"The Azores, officially the Autonomous Region of the Azores, is an archipelago of Portugal, in the Atlantic Ocean, about 1,400 km west of the Portuguese mainland. Politically and administratively, together with Madeira, it is one of the two autonomous regions of Portugal and a special territory of the European Union. It is the westernmost point and region of Portugal."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Flag_of_the_Azores.svg/330px-Flag_of_the_Azores.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Azores","wikidata":"Q25263"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Ribeira_Grande%2C_S%C3%A3o_Miguel_Island%2C_Azores_-_panoramio_%287%29_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Ribeira_Grande%2C_S%C3%A3o_Miguel_Island%2C_Azores_-_panoramio_%287%29_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Ribeira_Grande%2C_S%C3%A3o_Miguel_Island%2C_Azores_-_panoramio_%287%29_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Ribeira_Grande%2C_S%C3%A3o_Miguel_Island%2C_Azores_-_panoramio_%287%29_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Gaspar Frutuoso wrote Saudades da Terra, the first history of the Azores and Macaronesia, in the 1580s.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARibeira_Grande%2C_S%C3%A3o_Miguel_Island%2C_Azores_-_panoramio_(7)_(cropped).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/02/Henry_the_Navigator1.jpg/500px-Henry_the_Navigator1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/02/Henry_the_Navigator1.jpg/500px-Henry_the_Navigator1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Under the direction of Prince Henry the Navigator, the Azores were discovered and populated in the early 1400s.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHenry_the_Navigator1.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/58/Desembarcoislasterceiras.jpg/500px-Desembarcoislasterceiras.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/58/Desembarcoislasterceiras.jpg/500px-Desembarcoislasterceiras.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_c